tensity. I'll run a simulation with the release at an approximate point and check whether it reports enters_smoke.\n\nI'm planning to test a release near the target, using an updraft scaled by intensity (3.75 \u00d7 1.09 \u2248 4.09) since the simulator may only model the calibration fire. Because wind is uniform, I can translate the target to near the origin and check the offset, accepting some error in the column radius."}, {"before_call": 31, "text": "I'm checking the offset from release, about 88 m of x travel, and confirming the smoke radius tuning. I'm also questioning whether the release point at -100 is outside the smoke region, since the entry point matters for the path.\n\nI'm working out the release point: shifting it by about (+12, -16) would put the impact on the target, which gives roughly (-88, -6). Moving the release changes how the path crosses the smoke column, though, so rather than trust the shift I'll just simulate from that new release point."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "Adjusting the release point by (-89.4, -6.4) should bring the impact near the origin, which puts the real release at about (1029.6, -299.4). The nonlinearity is small, and a 1.5 m shift is acceptable. Updraft uncertainty of 3.5 to 4 might move the landing by one or two meters, which I'm treating as fine. Next I'm moving on to the second target at (-1150, 749), with I = 0.86, heading 239, and altitude 120.\n\nNow I'm checking the speed of 55 and the updraft at about 3.2 for the second target, and I'm estimating the release point as the impact minus the travel offset along the 239 heading. I'll run a simulation from a guessed release at (60, 40) to see where it lands."}, {"before_call": 34, "text": "For target 2, the release point is (40,25) and the impact lands at (51.5,-4.7), so the offset is (11.5,-29.7). The wind is almost cancelling the travel, so I need the release at (-11.5,29.7) to bring the impact to (0,0), and I'll simulate that. Target 3 is similar: release (25,-12)